<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="siroheme biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5194" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: SIROHEME Siroheme| is an iron-containing isobacteriochlorin, a modified tetrapyrrole similar in structure to both heme and chlorophyll that was discovered in 1973 |CITS: [4583265]|. Siroheme is covalently coupled to an iron-sulfur cluster ([FeS]) to form an electronically integrated metallo-cofactor for delivering electrons to substrate. Siroheme is found as a prosthetic group of several enzymes, including sulfite and nitrite reductases, which catalyze the six-electron reductions of sulfite to sulfide and nitrite to ammonia, respectively. Assimilatory sulfite reductases (see |FRAME: TAX-83333| |FRAME:SULFITE-REDUCT-ENZRXN|) are found in bacteria, fungi and plants but not in animals, while dissimilatory sulfite reductases are found in diverse sulfate-reducing eubacteria (see |FRAME: TAX-879| |FRAME:CPLX-266|) and some species of thermophilic archaebacteria (see |FRAME: TAX-2234| |FRAME: CPLX-7178|) |CITS: [8994874]|. Siroheme is also an intermediate in the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-17059| in denitrifying bacteria, and in an alternative |FRAME: Heme-b| biosynthesis pathway found in many archaea and sulfate reducing bacteria |CITS: [21969545]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Siroheme biosynthesis starts by the same route as other tetrapyrroles, the synthesis of the intermediate |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III| (see |FRAME:Tetrapyrrole-Biosynthesis &quot;tetrapyrrole biosynthesis pathways&quot;|). |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III &quot;Uroporphyrinogen-III&quot;| is an important branch point: its methylation directs it toward siroheme, cobalamin or factor F430 synthesis, while decarboxylation directs it toward the synthesis of chlorophyl and heme |CITS: [14633982]|. For siroheme synthesis, successive methylation at positions 2 and 7 forms |FRAME: CPD-9038| and |FRAME: DIHYDROSIROHYDROCHLORIN| (also known as dihydrosirohydrochlorin), respectively |CITS: [2407558]|. The enzyme that catalyzes the two methylation reactions, uroporphyrin III methyltransferase, requires |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| as a methyl donor, and has been characterized from several sources including bacteria |CITS: [1906874][2546914]|, archaea |CITS: [1856165]|, and higher plants |CITS: [8953249]|. To complete the synthesis of siroheme, |FRAME: DIHYDROSIROHYDROCHLORIN| is oxidized to |FRAME: SIROHYDROCHLORIN| and then chelated with ferrous iron. The first three intermediates are also intermediates in cobalamine biosynthesis (see |FRAME: PWY-5507|), and thus the committed step in the pathway is the ultimate one, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-4.99.1.4|. There are many variations of the enzymes catalyzing this pathway. In some bacteria (for example in the Gram-positive |FRAME: TAX-1423|) the transformation of |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III| into |FRAME:SIROHEME| is catalyzed by three separate enzymes (uroporphyrin III methyltransferase, dihydrosirohydrochlorin dehydrogenase and sirohydrochlorin ferrochelatase) |CITS: [12408752][10217486]|. In other organisms, such as the yeast |FRAME: TAX-4932|, the last two reactions are catalyzed by a single bifunctional enzyme |CITS: [11980703]|. Yet in other organisms, such as |FRAME: TAX-83333|, a single trifunctional enzyme (|FRAME:SIROHEMESYN-CPLX|) catalyzes all four reactions |CITS: [7945210]|. Organisms that use siroheme for |FRAME: Heme-d1| biosynthesis often have very different enzymes for synthesizing siroheme. For example, in P. denitrificans the &lt;i&gt;nirE&lt;/i&gt; gene, which is located in the heme &lt;i&gt;d&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; biosynthetic cluster, encodes the methyltransferase that catalyzes the first step, and a different ferrocheletase (Pdne_2332) that is not related to the cheletases mentioned above catalyzes the last step (the identity of the dehydrogenase is still not known in these organisms) |CITS: [24673795]|.</Description>
