    <DataNode elementId="UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III" textLabel="uroporphyrinogen-III" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="HUHWZXWWOFSFKF-UHFFFAOYSA-F" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="100.0" centerY="839.05859375" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco">|FRAME: CPD-11444 &quot;Uroporphyrinogen-I&quot;| and |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III| are both naturally occurring isomers. The difference between the two is the relative positions of the acetyl and propanoyl side chains on only one of the rings. Where in |FRAME: CPD-11444| the side chains of all four rings have the same relative positions, in |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III| the order is reversed on one of the rings. While |FRAME: CPD-11444| can be formed spontaneously from |FRAME: HYDROXYMETHYLBILANE|, the formation of |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III| requires a dedicated enzyme - |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.75 &quot;EC 4.2.1.75, uroporphyrinogen-III synthase&quot;|. Both isomers are substrates for |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.37 &quot;EC 4.1.1.37, uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase&quot;|, although |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III| is a better substrate |CITS: [6724109]|.</Comment>
